//@version=5
// =============================================================================
// Real-yield regime filter — drop-in snippet for the repaired strategy
//
// Gold pays no yield, so its opportunity cost is the real interest rate. The
// gold-bullish state is a FALLING real yield — the INVERSE of the price
// filter's "above its moving average". Getting that backwards is the easiest
// mistake here and it will silently halve your trade count.
//
// Backtested result (PREREGISTRATION.md, test 2): beat the 4H price macro
// filter in 5 of 6 walk-forward windows when used as a REPLACEMENT for it.
// Using both together was rejected (2/6) — they are substitutes, not
// complements. So `rate_mode = "replace"` is the tested configuration.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PASTE INSTRUCTIONS
//
// This is a fragment, not a standalone script. It references `macro_bullish`,
// which the host strategy defines from the 4H price filter. Pasting it into an
// empty chart window gives "Undeclared identifier: macro_bullish".
//
//   1. Paste everything between the INPUTS and COMBINATION markers into
//      RSI_Boll_Squeeze_repaired.pine, immediately AFTER the line
//          macro_bullish = higher_tf_close_l > higher_tf_sma_l
//   2. In the long entry condition, replace `macro_bullish` with
//      `macro_confirmed`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THERE IS NO MANUAL LAG INPUT (this was a bug in the first version)
//
// An earlier draft used `close[rate_lag]` inside request.security() to shift
// for FRED's publication delay. Two problems:
//
//   * Pine rejects a variable history offset inside request.security(), which
//     is the "unrecognised [rate_lag]" error — and because that line then fails
//     to compile, every later use of real_yield_ma reports as undeclared too.
//
//   * It was not needed. `barmerge.lookahead_off` on a "D" request already
//     means an intraday bar can only see the last CLOSED daily bar. During
//     day D you get day D-1. That IS the one-day lag; adding another
//     double-counted it.
//
// The Python backtest applies exactly one business day, so this matches it.
// (Robustness check: lags of 0, 1, 2, 3 and 5 days all score 4-5/6 — the
// result does not depend on this choice, which is what rules out a lookahead
// artefact.)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- INPUTS ----
use_rate_filter = input.bool(true,  "Use real-yield regime filter",
     tooltip="Gold rises when real yields fall. Requires the 10Y TIPS yield to " +
             "be below its own moving average.")
rate_symbol     = input.string("FRED:DFII10", "Real yield series",
     options=["FRED:DFII10", "FRED:DFII5"],
     tooltip="DFII10 = 10Y TIPS (real) yield — the tested series. DFII5 = 5Y, " +
             "more Fed-sensitive. Do NOT use a nominal yield such as DGS10 " +
             "unless you subtract breakevens yourself.")
rate_sma_len    = input.int(20, "Real yield MA length (days)", minval=2,
     tooltip="20 mirrors the 4H macro filter's length. Not tuned. Tested " +
             "plateau runs 10-50 days; it degrades at 5 and at 100.")
rate_mode       = input.string("replace", "Combine with 4H macro filter",
     options=["replace", "both"],
     tooltip="'replace' is the tested configuration (5/6 windows). 'both' was " +
             "rejected (2/6) — requiring both filters is over-restrictive.")

// ------------------------------------------------------------- THE SERIES ----
// One tuple request rather than two calls: half the security budget, and both
// values are guaranteed to come from the same daily bar.
//
//   "D"                  FRED series are DAILY ONLY — there is no intraday
//                        history. Requesting timeframe.period returns nothing.
//   barmerge.gaps_off    carry the last value through US market holidays.
//   barmerge.lookahead_off  MANDATORY. With lookahead_on you receive the daily
//                        close from the start of the day, which on an hourly
//                        chart is a time machine.
[real_yield, real_yield_ma] = request.security(rate_symbol, "D",
     [close, ta.sma(close, rate_sma_len)],
     barmerge.gaps_off, barmerge.lookahead_off)

// Falling real yield == gold-bullish. Note this is BELOW the average, the
// opposite of the price filter.
rates_bullish = not na(real_yield) and not na(real_yield_ma) and
                 real_yield < real_yield_ma

// ------------------------------------------------------------- COMBINATION ----
// `macro_bullish` comes from the host strategy's 4H price filter.
macro_confirmed = not use_rate_filter ? macro_bullish :
                   rate_mode == "replace" ? rates_bullish :
                   (macro_bullish and rates_bullish)

// Then in the long entry, swap `macro_bullish` for `macro_confirmed`:
//
//   if (ta.crossover(vrsiL, RSIoverSold) and ta.crossover(sourceL, BBlower))
//       and sqzUp and ta.crossover(LSMAS, LSMAL) and macro_confirmed
//       and trend_is_active and volatility_gate
//       strategy.entry("long", strategy.long, qty = long_position_size)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ PLOTS ----
plot(real_yield,    "Real yield (10Y TIPS)", color.orange, display=display.data_window)
plot(real_yield_ma, "Real yield MA",         color.yellow, display=display.data_window)
bgcolor(use_rate_filter and rates_bullish ? color.new(color.green, 92) : na,
     title="Real yield falling (gold-supportive)")

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- CHECKING ----
// 1. Open FRED:DFII10 as its own chart first and confirm it has history across
//    your backtest range. If it does not load standalone it will not load here.
// 2. Open the Data Window and check "Real yield" shows a number, not n/a.
// 3. Toggle `use_rate_filter` off and on. If the trade count does NOT change,
//    the series is returning na and the filter is silently inert — the same
//    class of bug as the original Keltner one.
// 4. Expect the green background roughly half the time; the tested series was
//    below its 20d average on 51.8% of bars.
